In mathematics, the term linear is used in two distinct senses for two different properties:
linearity of a function (or mapping);
linearity of a polynomial. An example of a linear function is the function defined by f ( x ) = ( a x, b x ) that maps the real line to a line in the Euclidean plane R2 that passes through the origin. An example of a linear polynomial in the variables X, Y and Z is a X + b Y + c Z + d.
